From: NanoKVM build <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] licheerv-nano: mux the IIC4 pads for the HDMI bridge

The LT6911C HDMI-to-CSI bridge is controlled over i2c4 at 0x2b. Enabling
i2c@4040000 in the kernel DTS is not enough: the pads also have to be muxed
to IIC4, and doing that through Linux pinctrl does not work on this SoC.

A DTS pinctrl group of

	pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_PWR_WAKEUP0, 5)>,	/* IIC4_SCL */
		 <PINMUX(PIN_PWR_BUTTON1, 5)>;	/* IIC4_SDA */

is rejected by pinctrl-cv18xx: the i2c controller then fails
pinctrl_bind_pins() with -EINVAL and never binds, leaving the board with no
/dev/i2c-4 at all. It fails silently -- nothing is logged at default level.
Bisected by stripping the group down: pinmux alone still fails, and removing
pinctrl-0 from &i2c4 entirely is what makes the controller bind. So it is the
pinmux entry that is refused, not the pin configuration. Every static check
that would explain it passes -- mux 5 is within the pad's mux_func_max of 7,
PINMUX() sets mux2 to PIN_MUX_INVALD as the driver requires, both pins are
present in the sorted pin array that sophgo_get_pin() bsearches, they share
an io-type and power domain, and both the sys and rtc register areas the
pads' conf lives in are mapped.

So do what the vendor does. Sipeed's own board init muxes these pads with
raw register writes rather than pinctrl (LicheeRV-Nano-Build,
build/boards/sg200x/sg2002_licheervnano_sd/u-boot/cvi_board_init.c):

	mmio_write_32(0x03001090, 0x5);	// IIC4_SCL
	mmio_write_32(0x03001098, 0x5);	// IIC4_SDA

0x090 and 0x098 are the FUNCSEL registers of PWR_WAKEUP0 and PWR_BUTTON1 in
mainline's pinctrl-sg2002.c, and CVITek's pad table agrees on the function
number (PWR_WAKEUP0__IIC4_SCL = 5, PWR_BUTTON1__IIC4_SDA = 5). Writing them
here leaves the pads already muxed when Linux starts, so the kernel node
needs no pinctrl-0 and never invokes the code path that refuses it.

Note these are the RTC-domain power-button/wakeup pads; muxing them to IIC4
gives up those functions. That is the routing this board uses -- i2c4 comes
out nowhere else on it.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [board-specific pad routing; upstream should
grow a working pinctrl description instead]
---
--- a/board/sophgo/licheerv_nano/board.c
+++ b/board/sophgo/licheerv_nano/board.c
@@ -5,10 +5,26 @@
 
 #include <dm/lists.h>
 #include <fdt_support.h>
+#include <asm/io.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 
 #include "ethernet.h"
 
+/*
+ * Pad function-select registers (pinctrl@3001000) for the two pads this board
+ * routes i2c4 to, and the IIC4 function number they take. See the patch header
+ * for why this is done here rather than through the kernel's pinctrl.
+ */
+#define PAD_FUNCSEL_PWR_WAKEUP0		0x03001090	/* -> IIC4_SCL */
+#define PAD_FUNCSEL_PWR_BUTTON1		0x03001098	/* -> IIC4_SDA */
+#define PAD_FUNC_IIC4			0x5
+
+static void licheerv_nano_mux_i2c4(void)
+{
+	writel(PAD_FUNC_IIC4, (void __iomem *)PAD_FUNCSEL_PWR_WAKEUP0);
+	writel(PAD_FUNC_IIC4, (void __iomem *)PAD_FUNCSEL_PWR_BUTTON1);
+}
+
 int board_init(void)
 {
 	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SYSRESET_CV1800B))
@@ -26,6 +42,13 @@
 	 */
 	cv1800b_ephy_init();
 
+	/*
+	 * Mux the IIC4 pads for the LT6911C HDMI bridge. Must happen before
+	 * Linux starts: the kernel's i2c4 node deliberately carries no
+	 * pinctrl-0, because pinctrl-cv18xx refuses this pad/function pair.
+	 */
+	licheerv_nano_mux_i2c4();
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
